"I'm the biggest loser around here," said the man in the next row, loud enough that they could both hear. He had the leather skin and vacant stare Dana associated with the chronic homeless.

Mei glared at him. She was always ready to take up the cudgels for anyone, which was part of why Dana loved her.

But it was the other woman's voice that was most startling. It vibrated like a plucked harp string. "Let her alone," the woman said. "Her kids are sick a lot. She can't miss story time."

Dana looked up. The woman was sitting in the second row, wearing a yellow jumper and enormous gold hoop earrings. There was a child snuggled against her shoulder, blanket swaddled, with a coughy wheeze.

"It's okay," Dana said. "I can do it."

"You got this," said Mei.

Dana fished the index cards from her lap and stood up. Her mind was blank except for one thought: Don't cry. But as soon as she started to speak her voice caught on the first sentence, and then the words came out in a gush.

"Sorry. I wrote this but I didn't-- I practiced but I wasn't really going to say it, but the thing is the library is important to my family. To all of us. My twins, they're only four. It's all they have. And the other kids, they have nowhere else to go. And Mrs. Ahn--she's... I don't even have words for how much she's helped. So please don't take away the Westside library. Please."

There was a pause. She fought the urge to bolt. A coughing paroxysm racked the sick child in the second row. The woman patted her shoulder, and the man muttered, "Jesus, kid, shut up already."

